nodecode-sabi is a rewrite of vizuh's vizuh/sabi:

  repository: https://github.com/vizuh/sabi
  commit: b59bd21b89fca091bf9ebcfb3082a4191e511da7
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Vizuh (LICENSE)

What was rewritten is the Hermes plugin in that repository
(packages/adapters/hermes/plugin: __init__.py and plugin.yaml), which was one
`llm_request` middleware: on a `sabi-code` Chat Completions round bound for the
configured loopback Sabi base, it set X-Sabi-Client to the client's own name
and passed on the event's opaque session and turn ids, replacing any stale copy
of those three headers and deriving nothing from prompt text. Here that becomes
a Nodecode provider cell: a catalog row for the alias, a public credential so
no key is asked for and no other family's variable is sent, and one advice on
the shared streaming walk that appends the same headers to a `sabi` round. The
client name is `nodecode`, this client's own, never another product's.

What was left out: the Hermes middleware seam itself (Nodecode advises the
provider walk instead), X-Sabi-Turn (no per-turn id is reachable at that seam,
so a round carries its session's id and nothing more), the setup wizard and the
profile files it generated, and the `SABI_HERMES_BASE_URL` variable, which the
cell's own section replaces. The original accepted only an exact loopback base
and refused everything else; this cell takes whatever `base_url` the section
gives, so a relay of Sabi can stand in for the proxy on the machine. No line of
the original's code is bundled or run: the plugin's Python is not a dependency
of this folder, which is Common Lisp against the Nodecode core alone.
